\ Fixture exercised by `make aot-asm-name-check` to verify that AOT-emitted C carries `asm("...")`
\ declaration attributes on the forward declarations of user-defined words, prelude words, and
\ compiled quotation bodies, and that the resulting linker symbols carry the same names.
\
\ Word names below intentionally include `?`, `-`, and `>` so the verbatim-preservation policy
\ is observable in both the saved C source and the binary's symbol table.

parse-json?: ( -- ) [ "ok" print-line ] ;
>foo: ( -- ) [ "in" print-line ] ;

\ The `if` arms below are compiled quotation literals. Each gets a separate emitted C function whose
\ symbol is named `pick-quot/quot@<line>:<col>` -- the `<line>:<col>` is the position of the opening
\ `[`. The two arms must produce distinct asm-names; they sit on separate source lines, so the line
\ part distinguishes them.
pick-quot: ( ? -- ) [
  [ "yes" print-line ]
  [ "no" print-line ]
  if
] ;

\ Twin one-line siblings, distinguishable only by column within the same source line. Statement-line
\ leading whitespace is stripped before tokenization, so columns are accurate within a line but not
\ across multi-line statements; this single-line case is the one that exercises true column-based
\ disambiguation.
flip: ( ? -- ) [ [ "lo" print-line ] [ "hi" print-line ] if ] ;

parse-json?
>foo
t pick-quot
f pick-quot
t flip
f flip
